# cargo-c -- builds and installs C-ABI artefacts from Rust crates, and exists in
# this tree for exactly one consumer: librsvg.
#
# librsvg's meson.build:27 is
#
#     cargo_c = find_program('cargo-cbuild', version:'>= 0.10.0')
#
# with NO required:false, so a missing cargo-cbuild is a configure failure
# rather than a degraded build. It is what turns librsvg's Rust into the
# librsvg-2.so, the .pc file and the gdk-pixbuf SVG loader that the rest of the
# desktop links.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env. The
# source is the crates.io crate, which is an ordinary gzip tarball despite the
# .crate extension -- fetch.sh's `tar -xf` detects that without help.
#
# The version carries cargo's own version as semver build metadata:
# 0.10.24+cargo-0.98.0. TAPEBUILD.toml records 0.10.24, because tape's resolver
# requires three-component semver and would SKIP THE PACKAGE SILENTLY if given
# the full string.
SRC_URL=https://static.crates.io/crates/cargo-c/cargo-c-0.10.24+cargo-0.98.0.crate
SRC_SHA256=e033742583695968c690582ab678f65c133779767940c9bc35718e990a235d45
SRC_DIR=cargo-c-0.10.24+cargo-0.98.0

# --features vendored-openssl IS MANDATORY HERE AND IS NOT A PREFERENCE.
#
# cargo-c depends on the cargo crate, which pulls openssl-sys, libgit2 and
# libcurl. Built WITHOUT this feature, the resulting binaries carry
# libcrypto.so.N and libssl.so.N in DT_NEEDED and must find that exact SONAME at
# RUN time, in whatever container receives them. That is precisely where this
# tree's two openssl majors disagree: the published index carries 3.5.7 while
# some build images carry 4.0.1, and a binary linked against one and run against
# the other fails to start with a message about a missing library rather than
# about cargo-c.
#
# With the feature, openssl is compiled into the binary and there is no runtime
# SONAME to disagree about. install.sh asserts that outcome from readelf rather
# than trusting the flag, AND checks its own detector against a binary that
# certainly does link openssl -- because a check that cannot fail proves
# nothing.
#
# --locked: the crate ships its own Cargo.lock. Without it cargo is free to
# REWRITE the lock when it considers it stale, silently resolving crate versions
# other than the ones this sha256 covers -- the same reasoning uutils-coreutils
# records, and the reason "pinned" is a fact here rather than a word.
CARGO_FEATURES="vendored-openssl"
